5.2 Estimated Financial Benefit

The main financial benefits of the system would come from reducing unnecessary purchases. When one department requires an item, the system may find a suitable asset that is no longer needed by another department.

Because the team does not have access to actual university purchasing records, the following calculation is based on planning assumptions. This example illustrates how the financial benefit might be calculated rather than a value that has already been realized.
For the first year's pilot, it is assumed that the system enables the university to reuse 10 assets. The average replacement cost is assumed to be AED 2,500 for each asset. An average repair cost of AED 250 and an average transfer cost of AED 100 are calculated for each reused asset.
Avoided purchase cost
10 assets × AED 2,500 = AED 25,000
Repair cost
10 assets × AED 250 = AED 2,500
Transfer cost
10 assets × AED 100 = AED 1,000
Net reuse saving
Net reuse saving = Avoided purchase cost − Repair and transfer costs
AED 25,000 − (AED 2,500 + AED 1,000) = AED 21,500
The estimated net reuse saving for the first year is equal to AED 21,500. Repair and transfer costs are subtracted from the financial benefit because they are not added back into the system cost table to avoid the same costs being counted twice.
5.3 Return on Investment
ROI formula
ROI = [(Total benefits − Total project cost) ÷ Total project cost] × 100
Using the values estimated for the one-year pilot:
ROI = [(AED 21,500 − AED 11,772) ÷ AED 11,772] × 100
ROI = 82.6%
Under these assumptions, the university would receive the following estimated financial gain during the first year:
AED 21,500 − AED 11,772 = AED 9,728
The average net saving provided by each reused asset can also be calculated:
AED 2,500 − AED 250 − AED 100 = AED 2,150 per asset
The approximate break-even point is:
AED 11,772 ÷ AED 2,150 = 5.48 assets
As a result at least six similar assets would need to be reused during the year for the estimated savings to cover the pilot cost.



5.4 Financial Feasibility Decision
The prototype is financially feasible since most of the development tools needed for its creation can be used for free. The prototype’s estimated cash cost is only AED 100 due to using employees’ existing laptops, development tools, GitHub Free, and a free database plan.
The one-year university pilot also seems to be financially feasible since the estimated net reuse saving of AED 21,500 is higher than the estimated pilot’s cost of AED 11,772, which yields an 82.6% ROI.

Nevertheless, the figures are only the planning estimates that should be used as a basis for the pilot deployment. The final financial feasibility will depend on the number of assets that will be reused, their types, conditions, and values. Therefore, before university deployment, the calculation should be updated using the actual asset purchase prices, repair costs, transportation expenses, expected number of users, and quotations approved by the university.
